[{:seon.task/name "terminal-verbs-answer-rows"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the agent's REPL read the program through search (ranked hits, one per definition, as maps), source (printed text to :out, never cut, 4-7k tokens a read on the scope bench) and docs/agent.md's hand recipes for grep -n, sed -n and tail over clojure.repl/source-fn. The sessions that built Seon read it through the shell instead (claude_scratch/context-lab/FINDINGS.md: across 150 recent sessions 74,714 Bash calls against 483 seon_eval; of 6,600 read pipelines grep starts 51%, sed 16%, 71% bounded, the common moves grep -> grep and grep -> sed). The owner, 2026-11-01: tools like the terminal's with the same meaning, explained in a line, answering vectors of maps the agent narrows itself, no automatic clipping under the reply budget (:seon.config.render.agent/token-budget, 16,000). What v1 did: no line-level verbs; its agents read whole sources. The change: ```my.agent/grep``` (every matching line over functions, tests, sections, task plans and schemas, {:at :kind :text}, :in and :ns, a string's words found through the search index ```seon.doc/text-index```, a regex reading every text), ```my.agent/ls``` (a namespace's definitions, a page's sections: ```seon.doc/outline```), ```my.agent/lines``` (sed -n over a var, section, page or task, numbered as grep numbers), ```my.agent/wc``` (tokens through the one printer). It replaces my.agent/grep's one hit per definition and the recipes in docs/agent.md#the-repl. Data in ```seon.doc/grep```. Measured next: an L1 first-eval probe, today's tools against these (claude_scratch/context-lab/PLAN.md, the terminal arm)."
